hi my name is Peter Dalton and I am the  technical director for Blue Point games  today I'm gonna take you on a journey as  we walk through Shadow of the Colossus  and sort of look at blue points approach  to remastering games we spent a lot of  time trying to do the right thing for  Shadow of the Colossus we're not trying  to reinvent it while we call it a remake  it's more of an extension of itself and  so I really feel like we try to pay  attention to the environments make sure  that we have the same vibe the same  language and at the end of the player  takes away the same sort of experience  playing the original game on the ps2 it  left certain memories in my mind and as  I come back I feel like we've created  the game that I remember playing the  very first time as we started digging  into the original ps2 version of the  game we started learning more and more  about it and it was actually very  interesting to find out the depth to  which the original team went to provide  details within the game take for example  it wasn't until later in development  that I even realized that the number of  birds that are in a scene are actually  dictated by the colossi number that  you're on oh the stairs the stairs are  so fun when we started this game we  found that very early that the size of  the stairs did not really match great  proportions to human size steps and so  wander felt like he was a  mountain-climber trying to climb over  the top of things and so we reworked the  staircases  however the minute you rework the  staircases you know they used to go  there and then you rework them so  they're not as tall and now all of a  sudden you you've got twice as far to go  it took quite a bit to get aggro to  actually behave correctly going down a  flight of stairs so it didn't look like  he was trumping as he stopped down the  stairs  one of the biggest challenges throughout  this entire game was our streaming  system basically breaking up the world  so we would go through and break up  areas within here to say here is here's  one area and then over here is a  completely  different area that's sort of like a  Vista and then behind you we'd have the  cathedral and break those all up into  streaming regions so that we could bring  them in and out this area here was quite  interesting because not only do we have  Vista stuff but we also have a lot of  detail that's happening down here with  waterfalls in the water flow and then  there there's always aspects of the game  that are very its they're difficult to  change in fact you don't want to change  them you don't want to lose the magic of  the original like aggro is one of those  cases aggro his AI he purposely does not  always listen to you which at times you  can find frustrating you can whistle and  call for him and he doesn't care and we  wanted to make sure that we didn't break  any of those behaviors other things we  are very strategic about what we pull  over  take wanders poncho for example the  original version needed love and needed  work and was built on ps2 technology we  reworked the entire poncho using  modern-day physics but we also if you  look at it we kept the bone joints that  hang around Wanderers waste those are  actually the original ps2 coder that  coded those up we left those alone  because I don't know who that guy is but  he's amazing we spent so much time  looking at how we build rocks in fact  it's kind of a joke at Blue Point of of  how many man-hours can you actually  spend building rocks you would think  that you would run out of rocks to  create but no apparently every rock  needs to be unique we have very large  rock kits where we would build these  faces of these cliffs throughout the  entire game and then you know sort of  piece everything together and take other  rocks and sort of wedge them in together  to basically create these outlines one  of the things that we realized we spent  a lot of time even very early on in the  game looking at vegetation the grass  that's inside of the environments as we  started to just place the grass around  the front cathedral area we quickly  found that unless we hire three or four  armies there's no way we'll ever  complete this game so we actually use a  vegetation system  where art will go through and they will  paint the density of the grass that they  want inside of an environment and then  will on the GPU actually read that  texture back and actually spawn the  grass dynamically so they have control  to go in and say I want this to be sort  of a thinner area in the environment and  over here I want to make sure this is  thick long lush sort of grass one of the  other additions that we added to the  game how do we take wander this  character and how do we make him feel  more attached to the environment how do  we make him feel like he's aware of  everything around him and so we actually  added head tracking that did not exist  in the original ps2 version as the  camera moves and looks throughout the  environment wanders head will actually  move and sort of track and he'll also  track additional things from lizards  that you'll find inside of the  environment he'll notice them a lot of  times before the player does you'll see  wander looking over at the corner and  wonder what is he doing well he knows  that there's a lizard over there or  there's fruit or as we start to come up  on a colossi you'll start to pay  attention that stuff is coming  let's talk about real quick another part  of the of our engine that I feel like  worked really well for the ps4 version  of Shadow of the Colossus is I remember  playing the the very first version of  Shadow of the Colossus at e3 and  standing behind I was in a line of three  people standing behind them watching  them play and then getting my turn but  the number one thing that really stood  out to me was what was ii think the size  and scope of the Colossus and then the  FIR  I thought the fur was amazing I've never  seen anything really at that granularity  of fur on a character I'd mentioned  earlier that we also have a system for  dynamic vegetation where artists would  paint on to the environment well as we  started getting further and further into  the game we realized that this Colossus  has vegetation on him he's got plant  life actually on him that moves and not  only does it need to move but it needs  to sway as the wind blows but still stay  attached and look like it looked  realistic  as we started progressing you know as we  brought the game up initially we started  with the ps3 version of Shadow of the  Colossus that we remastered several  years ago that was based on the ps2  version and as we started building on it  as we started environments you know all  the environments would start with the  original geo of the original game and  then we would slowly go through and  replace everything as we went along  and then as you would get the art to  come in and all the pieces to start to  form you start to notice that there's  just the little details are lacking to  wander not paying attention to where I  look to you know the the bubbles that  are released as wander swims around and  as we started adding all those little  things it was great because it sort of  motivated the team to really see the  attention to detail that needed to put  into this title and encouraged us to  push further and here's a good example  not only you've got a combination here  there's basically two different layers  working where you've got the fur on the  Colossus right here that blends in very  nicely to the actual the dynamic  vegetation system here with all the  plants that are actually on him  the fur was was a big undertaking and I  like to think that it came out really  cool as you most likely seen as wander  sort of climbs up on top of the colossi  we would put collision capsules on  wanderer to push the the fur and stuff  away as you climb up it we wanted to  make sure that the fur felt very organic  - art would spend hours going through  and actually painting the fur to dictate  the stiffness the length of the fur in  addition to the color  [Music]  the blackness that gets spewed as you  destroy a weak point on a colossus we  spent so much time trying to figure out  what that should actually look like and  when I say we spend a lot of time trying  to figure out what it looked like it's  really going back to the ps2 version  looking at videos after videos after  videos of how it's used in the  environment and trying to determine what  was the exact motivation and what was  this style that they were trying to  achieve so we've done everything from it  should be smokey - it should be inky -  the combination that we sort of have  here in the final product on a lot of  our previous projects that we had worked  on any time we do a remaster we would go  and work within the original codebase  that the original team provided and so  we would work on adopting their  processes and their art pipeline and  everything and so if there was a  behavior we wanted to change we would go  directly into their code and make that  change with shadow initially as we  started looking at it as a remaster we  thought okay maybe we'll do the same  thing the original ps3 version is much  more along those lines of taking the  shadow codebase and basically modifying  the shadow codebase to bring it from the  ps2 to the ps3 as we started diving  deeper and deeper into the ps4 version  of Shadow it became apparent that there  was not enough systems and other things  in place within the original codebase so  while we could have went in and added  all of those pieces it really just did  not make sense and so we sort of spent a  lot of time structuring and defining  with areas of responsibility so we have  the original codebase that's running the  entire time which is responsible for  things such as the Colossus behavior  he's the one that just called out for  the Colossus to shoot his projectile to  building that on top of the Blue Point  engine that basically sits underneath  that provides all of the rendering  abilities it's responsible for the fur  the water the particle simulations and  then we'll also go in and we create  these like little hybrid systems like  take wander for example the shadow  version of the engine  as a version of shadow inside of it that  communicates with a version of it inside  of the Blue Point engine and most of the  logic and behavior is defined within  shadow but then the Blue Point engine  will add additional things such as The  Wanderers poncho is completely within  the bpe space two additional foot I K  that will add to the game to the head  tracking that we talked a little bit  earlier you know num we would go through  and make minor adjustments you know just  quality of life sort of improvements on  each one of these environments there's  ledges everywhere that wander can  basically grab onto to traverse the  environment you know so we would go  through and look at angles of approaches  wander was approaching edges and easing  some of those up to make the game maybe  a little less stringent well also  internally debating about not making the  game too easy there's so many details in  this game it is part of what makes it  such a beloved game in fact there's a  guide no bad who goes through and has  broken down the entire ps2 version of  the game we actually reference his  website like perhaps more than we  actually should to understand the inner  workings of the entire game you know to  each after each of the Colossus is  defeated the area that in the sky that  shows up above them this sort of you  know calls them out in the environment  sort of reminding you what you've done  inside of the world we actually flew him  out to the office towards the end of  development to give him a chance we  really wanted to have him sit down and  play it he was kind of excited when he  played the development build and we  showed him that we could access the  debug menu and fly behind the scenes of  all of the environment that was there  which was very similar to how he had  sort of split up and cracked the ps2  version but we don't allow that anymore  we're coming up here this is one of my  favorite areas of the entire game this  forest towards the last maybe two three  months of development we added photo  mode into the game and if there's one  thing I could do again I would make sure  to add photo mode so much earlier on in  development but it was really cool  because the art staff the engineering  team QA  all the departments would have these  little contests where they would go  through the gameplay and come up with  the most interesting of photo mode  situations that they could come up with  and I remember this area like with all  the different photo mode filters that  you can add to change color balances to  black and white to adding film grain  over the top of the screen it just  created for some really great moments  that as you would share those amongst  each other you would be like wow how did  you make that happen the same thing was  very true like after we released the  game and I remember scrolling through  Twitter and looking at posts that users  had made and be like what game is that  oh wait wait that's the game that I just  worked on for so long okay that's pretty  cool talking about you know some of the  Colossus and you know the unique  challenges that every single one of them  provide we would we would take time to  go to the the ps2 version of the game  and extract all of the assets the models  the animations the skeletons and pull  those out of the original and use those  as the baseline for the ps4 version and  so one of the main things that our  animators would do would go through and  increase the bone counts on the  characters to basically create more  realistic motion in them  this guy was interesting because inside  of the code all of the bones were very  hard coded that there were exactly this  number of bones and then physics  deformation would basically be applied  to the bones to sort of get the up and  down swimming motion through this  Colossus has as it flies through the sky  and so things as simple as we would like  to add additional bones to increase the  fidelity of animation  and initially from engineering I would  say that it's absolutely acceptable  let's do that that sounds like a great  thing to do to the next day pulling up  the game and realizing that this colossi  is completely screwed and is in a ball  floating around in the sky how could you  foresee that it's been a decent amount  of time as well just like quality of  life sort of stuff  taking the bow aiming and improving it  so that it felt like it was a little  more fluid a little more natural well at  the same time we didn't want to change  it so it wasn't recognizable we made  changes such as changing the animations  if you if you play the game and notice  in the original ps2 version the  bowstring is always on the outside of  wanderer's arm well as we started  playing the game and you start looking  at details and paying attention to  what's actually going on you're like  it's actually quite awkward like nobody  actually carries a bow like that  so we reworked all of the animations  associated with the bow to basically  rework it so that the string is on the  inside of the arm so it's much more  natural to how an individual would  actually carry a bow I think Shadow has  taught me that once it comes out that's  really what you're gonna care about is  how much attention to detail did you  spend and how much care and love did you  actually put into it and so as I move  forward personally I want to make sure  that I'm putting in that that extra  little bit of love that extra little bit  of care paying a little bit more  attention to some of the more my new  details or quality of life things so  that when I'm looking at our game a year  after it's been released I can still  hold it up and say yes I did my absolute  best work there  [Music]

